A disability, stated plainly; a diagnosis, not stated at all
===========================================================

The persona used to answer "what disability does he have" with a diagnosis, and to explain the care
material as "how he got crazy". The second is nobody's business and the first is not what his own
statement says. What it says now is what he says: *he is disabled, on Social Security disability, and it
is the kind that screws with his speech and his memory.* That is the whole answer — the label is his to
give, and this is as far as it goes.

The About page keeps the sentence it should never have lost: this is assistive software, built by a
person who is disabled, because the standard tools assume words arrive whole and memory holds the thread.

Also here: **the project rules sit beside the work** — a `Project rules` column on the Projects page
reading `resources/project-rules.md`, the standing rules for this workspace (C++ and assembly first, Rust
once the design is settled and it is faster, flat arrays, real measurements, originals kept in
`legacy/`). A project can override it with its own `PROJECT-RULES.md`.

